 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to reporting on the status\nand outcomes of initiatives created in response to the heroin and opioid\nepidemic",
    "basePrintNo": "S4650",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4650-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §19.07, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4650\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to\nreporting on the status and outcomes of initiatives created in response\nto the heroin and opioid epidemic\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill requires the Office of Alcoholism and Substance Abuse Services\nto provide quarterly reports on the status and outcomes of various\ninitiatives to address the heroin and opioid epidemic.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one amends section 19.07 of the mental hygiene law by adding a\nnew subdivision (1).\n\nSection two establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nWith the opioid epidemic ravaging New York State, the need for well-ad-\nministered chemical dependence programming is immense. OASAS oversees a\nwide range of chemical dependence programming, ranging from direct\ntreatment services to long-term recovery housing. Despite their scope\nthough, there is very little public aggregation or tracking of the indi-\nviduals being served or the effectiveness of the programs themselves.\nIndividuals in chemical dependence programming are typically in an\nextremely vulnerable state, where the support they receive is critical\nto putting them on a pathway to long-term stability. We cannot serve\nthese individuals to the fullest extent possible without more robust\ntracking of program efficacy.\n\nThis legislation empowers OASAS to collect and report on the status and\noutcomes of initiatives created in response to the heroin and opioid\nepidemic. These quarterly reports will include information on the number\nof individuals enrolled in these programs, the number of individuals who\nhave completed the program, as well as other data to better understand\nthe populations being served. With a full picture of the services being\nprovided, we can finally make this housing the asset to our treatment\nand recovery arsenal that it ought to be.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-18: A.10177-A - Reported to Rules\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
